**Read:** The primary readings in [Residence time, activation and surface coverage](../../../utilities/industrial-gases-vacuum-and-high-purity-supplies/surfaces/Residence-time-activation-and-surface-coverage.lingua#r_48MYBH8H4756WWNK4ABCM2K1RT), especially the assumptions behind equivalent sites and first-order release.

Write a=sΓ/Ns and b=kdes for the shared single-site model, so dθ/dt=a−(a+b)θ under constant conditions. Its equilibrium is θ∞=a/(a+b) and its response is θ(t)=θ∞+[θ(0)−θ∞]exp[−(a+b)t], for a+b>0. If both rates are zero, coverage remains at its initial value.

For hypothetical a=0.02 s⁻¹, b=0.01 s⁻¹ and θ(0)=0, coverage after 100 s is about 0.63348. Reducing arrival changes the equilibrium, whereas merely waiting under unchanged conditions does not guarantee an empty surface. A monolayer fraction is also a different quantity from particle area coverage or a thick-film mass.

**Work:** Derive the solution with an integrating factor and verify it by independently integrating the differential equation. Explain why an equilibrium measurement alone identifies only a rate ratio. Combine a relaxation time with equilibrium coverage to infer the two rates, then state what evidence would reject equivalent sites, constant sticking or independent first-order release.

